## Overview
A production-grade Retrieval-Augmented Generation (RAG) agent that handles noisy customer support inquiries. It classifies intents against a rigid 6-class taxonomy, retrieves verified historical responses, and strictly escalates ambiguous or sensitive requests (e.g., security, financial) to a human agent, guaranteeing safety and reliability.

## Key Features
- **Fail-Closed Safety Routing:** Deterministic rules override LLM uncertainty to escalate sensitive or low-confidence requests.
- **Calibrated Classification:** Word/Char TF-IDF with Logistic Regression, calibrated via Sigmoid for honest probabilities.
- **Hybrid Retrieval:** Merges lexical BM25 with FAISS dense embeddings (`all-MiniLM-L6-v2`) via Reciprocal Rank Fusion.
- **Evidence Verification:** Automatically drops contradictory or irrelevant evidence before passing context to the generator.
- **Data Isolation:** Complete methodological separation of the 200-sample golden evaluation set from the 168k training records to eliminate data leakage.

## Architecture

```mermaid
graph TD
    A[User Query] --> B{Input Safety Guardrails}
    B -- Unsafe --> E[ESCALATE]
    B -- Safe --> C[Intent Classifier]
    C --> D{Policy Routing Engine}
    D -- Sensitive / Low Confidence --> E
    D -- Safe & High Confidence --> F[Hybrid Retrieval BM25 + FAISS]
    F --> G{Evidence Verifier}
    G -- Weak Evidence --> E
    G -- Strong Evidence --> H[LLM Generator]
    H --> I{Output Safety Gate}
    I -- Ungrounded --> E
    I -- Grounded --> J[AUTO_HANDLE Response]
```

## Tech Stack
**Backend:** FastAPI | **UI:** Streamlit | **ML:** Scikit-Learn | **Retrieval:** FAISS, sentence-transformers, rank-bm25 | **Testing:** pytest

## ⚡ Reproduce the Output (~5 min)

You can launch the dashboard and run evaluations instantly. Pre-built models and FAISS indices are already stored in `artifacts/`, so no retraining is required.

**1. Clone & Setup:**
```bash
git clone https://github.com/Dinesh-Sharma2004/Customer-Support-Agent.git
cd Customer-Support-Agent
python -m venv rag_env

# Windows
rag_env\Scripts\activate
# Linux/Mac
source rag_env/bin/activate

pip install -r requirements.txt
```

**2. Launch the Interactive Dashboard:**
```bash
python -m streamlit run scripts/streamlit_app.py
```
*The app will open at `http://localhost:8501` showing the agent's thought process.*

**3. Run Pipeline Tests:**
```bash
pytest tests/test_e2e.py -v
```

**4. Run Offline Evaluation:**
```bash
python scripts/run_evaluation.py
```
*Outputs will be saved to `artifacts/golden_evaluation_report.json`.*

## Results
Evaluated strictly against a locked, naturally imbalanced 200-sample golden set (`amazonhelp_golden_set_final.csv`), successfully isolating actual real-world performance from training leakage.

- **Classification Accuracy:** `0.7100`
- **Classification Macro-F1:** `0.6779`
- **Escalation Rate:** `71.5%` *(Successful fail-closed safety net)*
- **Auto-Handle Rate:** `28.5%` *(Confident & safe resolutions)*

## Project Structure
```text
├── artifacts/             # Pre-trained models, FAISS index, metrics
├── docs/                  # Deliverable methodology documents
├── scripts/               # Streamlit app, training, and evaluation scripts
├── src/
│   ├── api/               # FastAPI endpoints
│   ├── classification/    # Intent modeling & calibration
│   ├── retrieval/         # BM25, FAISS, and RRF logic
│   ├── safety/            # Guardrails and routing policies
│   └── pipeline.py        # Core orchestration logic
├── tests/                 # E2E adversarial tests
└── amazonhelp_golden_set_final.csv # Source-of-truth evaluation set
```

<details>
<summary><strong>Limitations & Next Steps</strong></summary>

- **Limitations:** The `0.71` classification accuracy stems from inherently noisy pseudo-labels in the training set. Dense retrieval currently struggles with highly specific domain acronyms (e.g., "VOD") out-of-the-box.
- **Next Steps:** Transition from heuristic pseudo-labels to an Active Learning setup to train the Logistic Regression baseline, and fine-tune the FAISS dense embeddings on historical Q&A pairs using `MultipleNegativesRankingLoss`.

</details>
